Committed sample of reference vectors (testbed/vectors-sample)

What this is. One reference vector for each of the 15 drivable targets of the
test bed (Phase 3, plan 03-08; CONTEXT D-21): the default configuration, the first
drivable entry by name, the smallest committed seed of that entry (ties by the seed's
sha256) and the first combination of the entry's scalar arguments. Each directory
<target>/default/<entry> is a one-vector corpus: <sha256>.json is the vector (the nine
fields of the schema, canonical JSON) and index.json is its provenance record: target,
configuration, entry, the hashes of the build objects, of the archive and of the driver,
the image digest, the executor and contract versions, the budgets, and the source of
has_ub, which stays "unchecked" ("UB unchecked", never "no UB"). The vectors say what
the original C does on the Cortex-M4 reference executor; they say nothing about any
translation of it.

Where the inputs come from. Every input is a seed this project already commits under
testbed/seeds/<target>, each directory with its own NOTICE and licence. No seed is
borrowed from another entry or another target (D-28), and nothing comes from the TRACTOR
corpus. The stdin of each vector is that seed file, byte for byte; a test
(eqv_04_committed_sample_is_valid) compares them.

Who produces the files. They are produced only by the testbed workflow, in the pinned image
(the toolchain image of container/README.md): the job "vectors" runs every seed twice
through the generated driver under qemu-arm, `mt vectors sample` chooses the vectors above from that corpus, and the
step "Fixture vectors sample" publishes them as a deterministic tar (gzip -n -9, base64)
in the notice "fixture vectors-sample", whose last line is the sha256 of the tar. The
files here are that tar after its sha256 was checked. They are never edited by hand, never
produced by a local run, and a regeneration replaces all of them from one CI run. The
same workflow replays them in every later run (the step "Replay the committed sample",
notice "vectors-sample") against the drivers it builds, and each vector must reproduce
byte for byte with the same driver, archive and object hashes.

